hi my name is franklin and today i'm  going to talk about translating chinese  into morse code  you're probably familiar with morse code  it was the way of sending messages on  telegraphs in the 1800s  the original encoding scheme really only  contemplated  um characters from the latin alphabet so  letters a through z  as well as numerals zero through nine  and it sent  each of these as a series of electrical  impulses that we represent here as dots  and dashes  as the telegraph network spread across  the world it was relatively  straightforward  and new encodings for different  alphabetic languages and so  you might have letters with diacritics  that would be slightly different  but in 1871 when hong kong and shanghai  were connected to the international  telegraph network  they had a problem that being  that chinese is a character-based  language and with  thousands and thousands of unique  characters and so the same strategy  doesn't really work um a telegraph  operator working in english  can memorize what a through z are and  can transcribe  messages as they come off the wire and  that would be unfathomable  unfathomable to do for something like  chinese  so what ended up happening is that these  first telegraph lines were built  by european companies being the  great northern telegraph company of  denmark and the eastern extension  anc telegraph company of the uk and  since they were european companies in  the 1800s they naturally chose some  europeans  to come up with the code there were a  couple of different iterations  uh the one that we're seeing here on the  right is the chinese telegraph code of  1871  uh which was put together by a french  customs officer  based in shanghai named vaguely  and the general approach was that they  took the most commonly used characters  and so starting with about 5 400 of them  although this grew up to about 6 800  um and they mapped each of them to a  four digit  code so zero zero zero zero through nine  nine nine nine  and if we look at a concrete example if  we take  the character dong which means winter if  you look that up  in a code book you'll see that it has  the value of 0392  which then you can use the like normal  international morse code representation  to to turn  into dots and dashes and  i built an app i have this link here  i'll i'll have it at the end as well  um that basically like steps through  through this and so  you can put in a bunch of chinese  characters and it'll give you  the corresponding morse code  um so  this has some implications you might  notice that with four digits we can  actually  represent 10 000 things and so we have  quite a bit of extra room  um these code books were arranged in a  way for search ability and so given  a character you could based on its  characteristics find the right place in  in the book  um and then look up what what the digits  were  um when they arranged the book they  ended up  leaving a bunch of extra spaces which  you can see here on on the right  um just in case in the future um they  needed  sort of to add new characters that i  came into vogue  um without having to kind of reshuffle  everything  the second implication is that uh it was  actually fairly slow  and verbose so if if we go back to  the um international morse code  you'll see that this is actually  arranged mostly for english letter  frequencies and so ease are very common  and  therefore very short whereas y's and z's  are quite a bit longer  and digits than themselves are pretty  long  um if you're trying to send a single  chinese character that is represented by  four zeros then you're on the hook for  20  long dashes and telegraph messages were  also priced by length and so  you would be paying for for all of that  so in addition there was this kind of  weird situation with plain text and  secret messages  so in english um telegraph operators had  developed a set of kind of special words  that were not  commonly used to to represent  longer phrases that they might come  across so if you got a message that said  toothbrush  what this actually meant was that your  telegraph uh your telegram had been  delayed in transmission and this was  great because it was less expensive it  was faster  but the telegraph companies were not a  huge fan because this cut into their  profits  so they developed a set of regulations  where  um plain text messages were priced  differently from secret ones  where the sort of like meaning wasn't  immediately obvious and  these prices were much higher per word  uh so as an english speaker like  uh you had a choice between do i want to  send this message  as plain text or in in secret  and while this originally had had  nothing to do with chinese  uh it was kind of caught in the  crossfire because chinese  was intermediated by these digits um  it had no choice but to be priced but  but to be lumped in with all of the  secret  messages which meant that it was even  more even more expensive  um this problem ended up going away  eventually uh  in 1893 the government of the chinese  republic  and the qing dynasty negotiated a  regulatory exemption  and made it so that if you were sending  a message  with chinese characters in a code book  um then it would be priced as as plain  text rather than as secret  which is an improvement um  so here we have the telegraph book or  sorry the telegraph code of 1871.  um it works but definitely has some some  drawbacks there were a bunch of  different ways that people tried to  improve on this and i'm going to dive  into one  which uh is referred to as trigraphs  so a trigraph is basically just a fancy  word for three alphabet letters  um as we noticed before digits are  pretty long in in morse code but letters  are much shorter  and so if we were able to map each  chinese character to a sequence of  letters then that would be  a lot faster and we can actually do  better because we don't need nearly as  many  uh letters uh we actually only need  three  which gives us more than enough uh space  to kind of represent all the letters  that we want  and so this is great going back to our  example of winter  the trigraph of apb is much shorter in  in morse code  and this isn't just a fluke if we jump  back to the website  we'll see that this poem originally in  in morse code  uh sorry in in digits when we switch  over to trigraphs  is like half the length  um and this is true for a bunch of  things  so that seems great  um there was a catch though so when this  was  developed originally latin characters  were not very widely understood  in china and so um the  code books had a mapping of latin  characters to chinese ones that were  phonetically similar  so instead of a b c you had i b  and c and that was great because it  meant that the operators could work  entirely in  in chinese they could look up the these  characters like like they were on the  right  um each of those boxes has the trigraph  kind of in the top left uh and then the  digit representation  in the bottom left and then the  character itself on on the right  um eventually um this mapping between  latin and chinese characters became less  necessary as  kind of exposure to to latin letters  uh was more widespread and so  eventually disappeared out of code books  and cool so to give a very concrete  example of  we have a phrase i like eating bows  which we have the conceptual equivalent  in chinese and we can see that you know  the digit representation in morse code  is fairly long  and with trigraphs it's uh actually like  probably just about the the same length  as the english phrase  which is pretty cool  we've talked about two ways that uh  chinese was encoded to morse code both  of these  had an intermediate step where they were  translated first to  digits or to trigraphs  and this extra step is is um still  common  um so 100 years later um  if you find yourself trying to type  chinese on a computer  or into your your phone it's actually  not that different  you you might be using some some type of  romanization  um and rather than a person searching in  a book for  the the right output it's um  it's your computer but we're still kind  of a step  a step removed um and chinese is  is weird and different when you compare  it to english or other alphabetic  languages  and so that's all i have um  thank you so much i encourage you to  check out the app and play around with  it  um for non-chinese speakers i have a  bunch of pre-loaded  phrases and poems that you can kind of  click around with and like get a sense  for for  how this all worked um i highly  recommend this book the chinese  typewriter  which goes into a lot more detail about  both like morse code stuff but also  just all the different ways that people  tried to create a typewriter for chinese  which has a lot of the same types of  problems  and yeah that's about it thanks
 

